<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.14. Bessel functions of the first kind</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <definition xml:id="def-bessel-function">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall that <term>Bessel's equation of order <m>n</m></term> is
        <md>
          x^2 y'' + xy'+(x^2-n^2)y = 0.
        </md>
        Solutions are found using the Method of Frobenius, that is assuming it has the form 
        <m>{y(x)=a_0 x^p + a_1 x^{p+1}+a_2x^{p+2}+\cdots}</m> for some unknown <m>p</m> and 
        solving for the coefficients.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <term>Bessel's functions of the first kind</term> we take <m>p = n</m>.
        We find that all odd coefficients are zero (<m>{a_1 = a_3 = a_5 = \ldots = 0}</m>),
        the coefficient <m>a_0</m> is a free variable, and the other
        even coefficients are 
        <md>
          a_{2m} = \frac{-a_{2(m-1)}}{4m(n+m)}
        </md>
        for <m>m=1, 2, 3, \ldots</m>. The solution to the order <m>n</m> equation is denoted <m>J_n(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find a solution to Bessel's equation of order 1
  <md>
  x^2 y'' + xy' + (x^2 - 1^2) y = 0
  </md>
  that is bounded at <m>x=0</m>.   Use Mathematica to draw a graph of it.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>We are not limited to integers. Find a solution to Bessel's equation of order 3/2
  <md>
  x^2 y'' + xy' + \left(x^2 - \left(\frac32\right)^2\right) y = 0
  </md>
  that is bounded at <m>x=0</m>.   Use Mathematica to draw a graph of it.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    <em>Fun facts!</em>  
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Bessel functions of integer order show up when solving problems in cylindrical 
    coordinate systems, and but half-integer orders of Bessel functions (<m>n=m+\frac12</m>)
    appear in spherical problems.  
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          When solving Bessel's equation of order <m>n</m>, if we take <m>p=-n</m>
    then we will find <term>Bessel's functions of the second kind</term>, which we denote <m>Y_n(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Hankel functions</term> are complex functions that come from combining Bessel functions of the first and second kind, 
          so <m>H_n^{(1)} = J_n+iY_n</m> are Hankel functions of the first kind, and <m>H_n^{(2)} = J_n-iY_n</m> are 
          of the second kind. (We won't get into these...)
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

</worksheet>
